#source(235, "223")
#heading(level: 1)[Open Problems] <ch:open-problems>

In conclusion we draw the reader's attention to some open problems which, in our
view, are of interest for function theory and combinatorial analysis.

In @ch:integral-method[Chapter] the method of integral representation of sums
was constructed, using power series, as a method for finding integral
representations of combinatorial sums and evaluating them. It was justified by
the theory of one-dimensional and multiple residues, and some applications were
given in Chapters @ch:univariate-sums–@ch:applications.

It is natural to extend the method to generating functions and integrals of
other types, following the same scheme and seeking further applications. We
formulate some problems.

#problem(base-level: 1, prefix: ())[
  Construct a method of integral representation of sums according to the scheme
  of @ch:integral-method[Chapter] for generating functions
  $A(z)=sum_(n=0)^infinity a_n phi_n (z)$ defined, for example, by the following
  sequences of functions.
] <prob:generalized-series-method>

#subproblem[
  $
    phi_n (z)=z^n/((z-1)(z-2) dots (z-n)), quad n=1,2,dots, quad phi_0 (z)=1
  $
  #bib-ref("Carlitz1973b").
] <prob:factorial-rational-series>

#subproblem[
  Euler series of power type #bib-ref("Goldman1970"),
  $
    phi_n (z)=z^n/((1-q)(1-q^2) dots (1-q^n)), quad n=0,1,2,dots,
  $
  where the empty product is $1$ and $q^j != 1$ for all $j>=1$.
] <prob:q-power-series>

#subproblem[
  Interpolation series, especially series in generalized powers,
  $
    phi_n (z)=(z(z-1) dots (z-n+1))/(n!), quad n=0,1,2,dots,
  $
  with $phi_0 (z)=1$.
] <prob:interpolation-series>

#subproblem[
  Dirichlet series.

  #source(236, "224")
] <prob:dirichlet-series>

#subproblem[
  Fourier series with an orthonormal system $\{phi_n (z)\}_(n=0)^infinity$,
  especially trigonometric series and orthonormal systems of polynomials.
] <prob:fourier-series>

#subproblem[
  Other series (see #bib-ref("Doubilet1972b") and
  §@sec:rota-generating-functions).

  In these constructions, obtain analogues of the Bürmann–Lagrange series for
  inverting an implicitly defined function, whenever this operation is
  admissible in the algebra of series under consideration. Justify the
  constructions by methods of function theory.
] <prob:incidence-algebra-series>

#problem(base-level: 1, prefix: ())[
  Construct a multidimensional method for the series listed in
  @prob:generalized-series-method, following the scheme of
  @ch:integral-method[Chapter] and using multidimensional residues. Find
  inversion formulas for systems of implicitly defined functions in these other
  classes of series.
] <prob:multivariate-series-method>

#problem(base-level: 1, prefix: ())[
  Construct a method for generating functions of two or more variables of
  “mixed” type, especially functions of the form #eq(
    $
      A(w,z)=sum_(n=0)^infinity sum_(m=0)^infinity a_(n m) z^n phi_m (w),
    $,
  ) <eq:generalized-generating-series>
  where $\{phi_m (w)\}_(m=0)^infinity$ is an orthonormal system. The
  coefficients identify such expansions with infinite matrices. On a class where
  matrix composition is defined, for example the row-finite matrices, this
  correspondence transports matrix multiplication to a product of generating
  functions. This product generally differs from pointwise multiplication.
] <prob:generalized-generating-series>

#problem(base-level: 1, prefix: ())[
  When Problems
  @prob:generalized-series-method[]–@prob:generalized-generating-series[] have
  positive solutions, extend the method's uniform approach to finding integral
  representations and evaluating the following sums:

  4.1. sums with $q$-binomial coefficients;

  4.2. sums over partitions;

  4.3. sums over divisors;

  4.4. infinite sums generated by Fourier series;

  4.5. sums of other types.

  Seek new multiple summation formulas and analogues of known power-series
  summation formulas, with particular attention to sums arising directly in
  combinatorial applications.
] <prob:generalized-identities>

#problem(base-level: 1, prefix: ())[
  Write analogues of the integral representations for the general term of a
  $2n$-dimensional matrix of type $F_n^1$ (see §@sec:univariate-inversion) in
  the algebras of series in Problems
  @prob:generalized-series-method[]–@prob:generalized-generating-series[].
  Determine how these matrices are connected with known summation methods for
  divergent series (see, for example, #bib-ref(
    "Hardy1949",
  )) and
  #source(237, "225")
  with known discrete probability distributions. Obtain analogues of the
  decomposition and inversion theorems. Write analogues of the inverse pairs in
  §@sec:univariate-inversion for matrices of type $F_1^1$. Give combinatorial
  interpretations of the results (see, for example, #bib-ref("Good1965"),
  #bib-ref("Chottin1975") and #bib-ref("Tutte1975")).
] <prob:generalized-matrix-inversion>

#problem(base-level: 1, prefix: ())[
