#problem(base-level: 1, prefix: ())[
  Find conditions on the numerical sequences $\{alpha_m\}_(m=0)^infinity$ and
  $\{beta_k\}_(k=0)^infinity$ and the analytic functions $g(w)$, $f_1 (w)$ and
  $psi_1 (w)$ under which a matrix of type $F_1^1$ is: a) stochastic; b) doubly
  stochastic. Find multidimensional analogues for matrices of type $F_n^1$.
] <prob:regular-summation-methods>

#remark[
  For an infinite row-stochastic matrix $(C_(m n))$, the associated linear
  summation method is regular, that is, preserves the limits of convergent
  sequences, if and only if $C_(m n)->0$ as $m->infinity$ for each fixed $n$.
  Nonnegativity and unit row sums alone do not imply regularity.

  Matrices of type $F_1^1$ include Gronwall's summation matrices, which give
  many regular summation methods under suitable additional analytic conditions.
  The Gronwall matrix acts on the partial sums of a series and is lower
  triangular, with general term #eq(
    $
      C_(m n) & =res_w (g(w) f(w)^n (1-f(w)))/(b_m w^(m+1)) \
              & =1/(2 pi i b_m) integral_(abs(w)=rho)
                (g(w) f(w)^n (1-f(w)))/(w^(m+1)) dif w,
    $,
  ) <eq:summation-matrix-kernel>
  #index-mark([@eq:summation-matrix-kernel], group: [Methods])[Gronwall
    summation]
  where $m,n=0,1,2,dots$, $g(w)=sum_(m=0)^infinity b_m w^m$, $b_m!=0$ for all
  $m$, $f(0)=0$ and $f'(0)!=0$. The positively oriented circle lies in a disk
  where $f$ and $g$ are holomorphic.

  A. A. Melentsov's Doctoral Dissertation (Scientific Library, Ural State
  University, Sverdlovsk) investigates this method and its generalizations for
  $n=1$, starting from the properties of the holomorphic functions $f$ and $g$.
]

#problem(base-level: 1, prefix: ())[
  Suppose that a matrix of type $F_n^1$ is given (see @def:matrix-integral).
  Write $m=(m_1,dots,m_n)$ and $k=(k_1,dots,k_n)$. Let $g(w)$,
  $f_1 (w),dots,f_n (w)$ and $psi_1 (w),dots,psi_n (w)$ be holomorphic in a
  neighbourhood of the closure of a polydisk $U(rho)$ with skeleton
  $
    Gamma(rho)=\{w in CC^n: abs(w_j)=rho_j, quad j=1,dots,n\}.
  $
  #source(238, "226")
  Associated with this matrix are the numerical sequences $\{p(m)\}$ and
  $\{q(k)\}$:

  #keep-next[a) $p(m)=p(m_1,dots,m_n)$:]
  #eq(
    $
      p(m) & =1/((2 pi i)^n beta_k) integral_(Gamma(rho)) g(w) \
           & quad times product_(j=1)^n
             (f_j (w)^(m_j)(1-f_j (w)) w_j^(-k_j-1)) dif w,
    $,
  ) <eq:multivariate-summation-kernel>
  where $m_1,dots,m_n=0,1,2,dots$, the $k_j$ are fixed positive integers, and
  $abs(f_j (w))<1$ on $Gamma(rho)$ for every $j$.
  $
    beta_k=(2 pi i)^(-n) integral_(Gamma(rho)) g(w) w^(-k-I) dif w != 0.
  $
  Sufficiently small radii give the bound whenever $f_j (0)=0$. Summing the
  geometric series under the integral gives $sum_m p(m)=1$; nonnegativity is a
  further requirement.

  #keep-next[b) $q(k)=q(k_1,dots,k_n)$:]
  #eq(
    $
      q(k)=1/((2 pi i)^n) integral_(Gamma(rho)) g(w)
      product_(j=1)^n (psi_j (w)^(k_j) w_j^(-k_j-1)) dif w,
    $,
  ) <eq:multivariate-transform-kernel>
  where $k_1,dots,k_n=0,1,2,dots$ and $psi_j (0)!=0$ for $j=1,dots,n$. Under
  suitable sufficient conditions on $g$ and the $psi_j$, this sequence defines
  the one-dimensional discrete Lagrange distribution or its multidimensional
  generalization introduced by Consul and Shenton in #bib-ref("Consul1972") and
  #bib-ref("Consul1973"). These families include a number of known discrete
  distributions.
] <prob:multivariate-summation-kernel>

#subproblem[
  Find conditions on $g$ and $f_1,dots,f_n$ under which
  @eq:multivariate-summation-kernel defines a probability distribution.
] <prob:probability-kernels>

#subproblem[
  Find integral, closed-form (if possible), and asymptotic representations for
  the moments of a distribution of the form @eq:multivariate-summation-kernel,
  together with local and integral limit theorems for distributions of the forms
  @eq:multivariate-summation-kernel and @eq:multivariate-transform-kernel. In
  particular, solve these problems for distributions of the form
  @eq:multivariate-summation-kernel obtained from the matrix of the method
  $P(q,r,s)$, which is of type $F_1^1$ (see @prob:summation-relations).
] <prob:distribution-moments>

